<title>RUSSIA: Armenia may seek U.S. grain credit for 70,000 T.</title>
<headline>Armenia may seek U.S. grain credit for 70,000 T.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1996-12-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Armenian government officials are discussing the possibility of securing a $15 million credit from the U.S. to buy 70,000 tonnes of food grain, Interfax news agency said on Monday.</p>
<p>It did not say whether the former Soviet republic was in direct talks with the U.S. Department of Agriculture's Commodity Credit Corporation, which would extend the credit.</p>
<p>If a credit were extended, first deliveries would begin in 1997, Interfax said.</p>
<p>Armenia's grain harvest rose this year to 340,200 tonnes from 1995's 271,000.</p>
<p>Interfax said annual demand was about 400,000 tonnes.</p>
<p>--Moscow Newsroom +7095 941 8520</p>
</text>
